# 4. Treatment Planning (NCE content outline, 9%): 20 items
CAT = "ncePlan"; SEG = "PLN"
Q = [
("Which treatment goal is written in SMART form?",
 "Attend 3 meetings a week for 8 weeks", ["Feel much better about life soon", "Stop being so anxious in daily life", "Work on self-esteem issues"],
 "SMART goals are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound. Feeling better or working on self-esteem cannot be measured as written."),
("How does an objective differ from a goal in a treatment plan?",
 "It is a measurable step to the goal", ["It is broader than the goal", "It is written only by the client", "It replaces the client's diagnosis"],
 "Goals are broad outcomes, such as reduced depression. Objectives are the specific, measurable steps that show progress toward them."),
("Who should set the goals in a client's treatment plan?",
 "The client and counselor", ["The counselor on her own", "The client's insurance company", "The client's family members"],
 "Collaborative goal setting increases the client's ownership and motivation. The counselor brings clinical knowledge and the client brings priorities."),
("A client has several problems: panic attacks, a job search and marital strain. What should guide which one is addressed first?",
 "Severity and the client's priorities", ["The easiest problem to solve first", "The counselor's own favorite clinical area", "The order the client listed them"],
 "Treatment plans usually address safety and the most distressing or impairing problems first, weighed against what the client most wants to work on."),
("A court-ordered client says his drinking is not a problem and he has no plans to change. Which approach best fits his stage of change?",
 "Raise awareness without pushing change", ["Set a firm quit date today", "Teach relapse prevention skills", "Assign a daily sobriety log"],
 "A client in precontemplation is not yet considering change. Stage-matched work builds rapport and raises awareness, for example with nonjudgmental feedback, rather than pushing action steps he has not chosen."),
("A client has set a quit date for next week and asks what to do next. Which counselor task best fits this stage of change?",
 "Build a specific change plan", ["Debate whether he has a problem", "Wait until the urges have passed", "Review his gains from past years"],
 "In preparation the client intends to act soon, so the counselor helps turn intention into a concrete plan: steps, supports, likely obstacles and how to cope with them."),
("A client says, \"Part of me wants to stop drinking, but it is the only way I relax.\" Which counselor response best fits this stage of change?",
 "Explore both sides of his ambivalence", ["Hand him a detailed quit plan", "Tell him he is in denial", "Praise his long-term sobriety"],
 "This client is in contemplation: he sees reasons to change and reasons not to. Exploring the pros and cons of change, and reflecting his own reasons for it, helps resolve ambivalence before planning."),
("A client's depression is not improving after several months of weekly sessions. What should the counselor do?",
 "Review and revise the plan", ["Keep the same plan unchanged", "End services without notice", "Double the length of sessions"],
 "Treatment plans are living documents. Lack of progress calls for reassessing the diagnosis, goals and interventions, consulting, and possibly referring."),
("Which level of care is the least restrictive for a client who is stable and attends work?",
 "Routine outpatient counseling", ["Residential treatment", "Partial hospitalization", "Inpatient hospitalization"],
 "Clients are placed in the least restrictive level of care that keeps them safe and meets their needs. Routine outpatient care is the least intensive."),
("A client with severe depression is considering medication. What is the counselor's role?",
 "Refer to a prescriber", ["Recommend a specific medication", "Advise against all medication", "Adjust the client's dose"],
 "Counselors do not prescribe. They refer to a psychiatrist or other prescriber and coordinate care with the client's written consent."),
("When does discussion of termination ideally begin?",
 "Early in counseling", ["In the final session", "Only if the client asks", "After the last payment"],
 "Discussing the expected length and end of counseling from the start prepares the client, and the plan sets the criteria for ending."),
("A client is ready to end counseling. Which step helps her keep her gains?",
 "Plan for future setbacks", ["Stop all coping strategies", "Avoid talking about progress", "End without a final session"],
 "Relapse prevention identifies warning signs, high-risk situations and coping steps, so the client can maintain progress after counseling ends."),
("A client with a substance use disorder uses once after months of sobriety. In relapse prevention, this lapse is best treated as:",
 "A learning opportunity", ["Proof that treatment failed", "A reason to end counseling", "A sign to change counselors"],
 "Relapse prevention treats a lapse as a chance to learn triggers and strengthen coping, which reduces the abstinence violation effect of shame and giving up. A lapse is a single slip; a relapse is a return to the old pattern of use."),
("A client completes residential treatment. What should the discharge plan include?",
 "Follow-up care and supports", ["Only the final bill", "A list of her past mistakes", "A promise of no future help"],
 "A discharge or aftercare plan links the client to outpatient care, community supports and steps to take if symptoms return."),
("A counselor's client is also seeing a psychiatrist. With the client's consent, the counselor should:",
 "Coordinate care with the psychiatrist", ["Avoid all contact with the psychiatrist", "Share notes without consent", "Ask the client to choose one provider"],
 "Concurrent treatment works best with coordination. The counselor gets a signed release and keeps both providers working toward shared goals."),
("A group member is leaving next week. How should the leader handle the transition?",
 "Let members discuss it", ["Say nothing to the group", "Replace the member that day", "End the group early"],
 "Departures affect the whole group. Giving time to say goodbye and process feelings supports cohesion and models healthy endings."),
("A client's career test results show strong interests in health care. How should the counselor use them?",
 "Discuss them to aid her decision", ["Tell the client which job to take", "File them without discussion", "Share them with her employer"],
 "Assessment results are used with the client to inform decision making, not to make the decision for the client."),
("Which client strength is most useful to name in a treatment plan?",
 "A supportive sister nearby", ["A history of panic attacks", "A recent job loss", "A family history of depression"],
 "Strengths such as social support, past coping and motivation make goal attainment more likely and are built into the plan."),
("A client keeps missing sessions because of unreliable transportation. In treatment planning this is best identified as a:",
 "Barrier to goal attainment", ["Sign of client resistance", "Reason to end counseling now", "Diagnostic symptom"],
 "Plans name barriers such as transportation, cost or childcare so the counselor and client can solve them, for example with telehealth or bus passes."),
("A client finishes counseling. A month later the counselor checks in as planned. This follow-up mainly serves to:",
 "Check that gains are holding", ["Bill for one more session", "Restart weekly counseling", "Collect a client testimonial"],
 "Follow-up after discharge shows whether progress is maintained and offers a path back to services if the client needs them."),
]
